(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-ent-analgesics-quiz"]={"title":"Analgesics & Anti-inflammatories Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Aspirin produces different effects as exposure rises. In what order do they appear?","choices":["Anti-inflammatory, then analgesic, then antiplatelet","Antiplatelet, then analgesic and antipyretic, then anti-inflammatory","Analgesic first, then antiplatelet, then anti-inflammatory","All three appear together at any exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One drug with three jobs stacked by exposure, which is why 'taking aspirin' means very different things in different patients. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is a patient on aspirin told about pain that does not settle?","choices":["To follow up if it worsens or persists","To double the amount taken","To switch to an anticoagulant","To continue taking it indefinitely if it helps"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is a time limit on treating yourself, and it exists so that a persistent cause gets looked at rather than masked. The limit given is about ten days. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What should a patient who drinks alcohol regularly be told about acetaminophen?","choices":["Alcohol prevents acetaminophen from working","The combination causes gastric ulceration","There is no interaction between the two drugs","The combination risks liver damage over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neither is dangerous on its own at ordinary use, which is what makes the combination easy to arrive at without noticing. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes naproxen from ibuprofen?","choices":["It is selective for COX-2","It has no gastrointestinal effects","A longer half-life","It is available only on prescription"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same class and same problems, but a longer half-life, which matters for adherence more than for efficacy. So it is taken less often. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A child has a fever during chickenpox. Why must aspirin be avoided?","choices":["It is ineffective against viral fever","It causes irreversible hearing loss in children","It raises the risk of Reye's syndrome","It interacts with the varicella vaccine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A viral illness plus aspirin in a child is the exact combination that produces it, which is why the rule is about the setting rather than the drug alone. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A patient taking aspirin regularly develops ringing in the ears. What does that suggest?","choices":["Exposure has reached the anti-inflammatory range","An unrelated sensorineural hearing loss on that side","An allergic reaction to aspirin","The dose is too low to be effective"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinnitus is the audible marker that the patient has climbed the ladder from analgesia into the anti-inflammatory range, which makes it useful rather than incidental. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A child has fever during influenza. Why is acetaminophen preferred over aspirin?","choices":["Acetaminophen is anti-inflammatory and aspirin is not","Aspirin in a child with viral illness risks Reye's syndrome","Aspirin does not reduce fever","Acetaminophen also treats the influenza itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the everyday consequence of the Reye's association, and it is why acetaminophen became the default antipyretic in children. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does ibuprofen differ from aspirin at cyclooxygenase?","choices":["It inhibits reversibly rather than irreversibly","It inhibits COX-2 only","It does not inhibit cyclooxygenase","It inhibits irreversibly, but only COX-1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The effect lifts as the drug clears, which is why ibuprofen is not used as an antiplatelet the way aspirin is. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which patients should avoid ibuprofen?","choices":["Those with asthma it may worsen","Those with a history of Reye's syndrome","Those taking acetaminophen","Those over sixty-five years old"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is the one most easily forgotten, because it is not an obvious consequence of blocking prostaglandin synthesis. Previous ulcer or perforation, or renal impairment. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is ibuprofen a problem for a patient taking methotrexate?","choices":["It displaces methotrexate from its receptor","It prevents methotrexate from being absorbed","It inactivates methotrexate in the liver","It reduces methotrexate secretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Same mechanism as the lithium interaction -- reduced renal clearance of a drug that does harm when it accumulates. Producing toxic levels. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Aspirin is contraindicated in pregnancy, yet one exception is noted. What is it?","choices":["It is safe throughout the third trimester","It may be used to treat fever during pregnancy","It may be used if the patient is also on an anticoagulant","Very low exposure may benefit pre-eclampsia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The exception is narrow and specific, and it is the reason the contraindication is stated as a rule with a footnote rather than as an absolute. That is, the hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which features characterise Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Fever, a rash and migratory joint pain","Vomiting, neurological damage, liver injury and low glucose","Jaundice with painless distension of the abdomen","Isolated hepatic failure without neurological signs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The hypoglycaemia is easy to forget alongside the liver and brain findings, and it is treatable. The neurological damage is progressive, and the glucose is genuinely low rather than borderline. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which illnesses typically precede Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Bacterial otitis media or sinusitis","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Gastroenteritis from food poisoning","An upper respiratory infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All viral, which is why the aspirin restriction in children is framed around fever from viral illness specifically. Influenza or chickenpox. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is known about how acetaminophen works?","choices":["Its mechanism is not fully established","It irreversibly inhibits COX-1 in the periphery","It blocks prostaglandin receptors directly","It acts through opioid receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is worth saying plainly rather than inventing an explanation: the mechanism is not fully worked out, and the drug is used on its observed effects. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is Reye's syndrome also called, and what does the name describe?","choices":["Salicylate hypersensitivity syndrome","Acute hepatic porphyria","Fatty liver encephalopathy","Viral haemorrhagic encephalitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The alternative name is a compact description of the illness: liver and brain together. Naming the two organs involved. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is a patient on aspirin told not to take it with an anticoagulant?","choices":["The anticoagulant blocks aspirin's absorption","Aspirin makes the anticoagulant ineffective","Aspirin inhibits platelet function","The combination causes Reye's syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two different parts of haemostasis are disabled at once, which is more than the sum of either alone. Adding a second bleeding mechanism. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A patient takes aspirin for fever. When are they told to come back straight away?","choices":["If they feel drowsy after the first dose","If the fever does not come down","Only once a full week has passed","If the fever returns at any point"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The instruction is about the fever failing to respond, which is a different signal from the fever simply continuing. That is, if it fails to respond to the medicine rather than merely persisting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
